timestamp=2026-11-04T19:31:35.508Z level=INFO run=90d54173 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# cases with only one type, but thousand-like? e.g. 1000 vs 10.00\n# Rule 6: If only one type of separator exists, treat it as a decimal separator only when exactly two digits follow its last occurrence.\n# 1.000 has dot, 3 digits follow -> so treat as decimal? No wait: \"treat it as a decimal separator only when exactly two digits follow its last occurrence\"\n# So for 1.000: 3 digits follow dot -> treat as NOT decimal? So it becomes 1000 (remove as thousand separator?) Or treat as decimal means keep it. Hmm look at the expected meaning.\n# In finance, 1.000 could mean 1 thousand (european uses dot as thousand) or 1.00 (US uses dot as decimal). Also 1,000 vs 1,00. The rule gives \"decimal separator only when exactly two digits follow its last occurrence\"\n# So if only one type exists: if it has 2 digits after, treat it as decimal separator (so keep it). If not 2 digits, treat it as thousand separator (so remove it).\n# That gives: 1.000 -> 3 digits after dot -> not exactly 2 -> treat as thousand sep? so remove dots -> 1000. That matches our current results: we got 1000.0. Correct.\n# 808.824 -> 3 digits after, remove -> 808824. That matches. Also in US, 808.824 is unusual as decimal; could be thousand groups? Or maybe just a number with 3 decimals. But we must follow the stated rule.\n# And \"C1 1.000\" gave us 1000.0 which matches if dot is thousand sep in that context? Or decimal? According to rule, 3 digits after -> treat as thousand sep, remove -> 1000. Correct per rule.\n# Also \"C1 1,234.56\" - both exist, dot is last -> treat as decimal, so remove commas (thousand) -> 1234.56. Correct.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
