timestamp=2026-11-04T19:43:56.942Z level=INFO run=90d54173 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# So for R00212, the two None-dt rows: idx 13 gives net 154.22, idx 1518 gives 191.08. Both have real net. The rule says: \"breaking ties by preferring the version with a real net result, then earlier file order.\"\n# Both have real net - so first tie-breaker (real net result) doesn't choose; both same. Second tie-breaker: \"earlier file order\" - so we pick idx 13 (smaller). That gives us the row with 154.22.\n# That looks plausible. But is this the \"best\" choice overall as per the full spec for deduplication? The rule as stated: latest event_time; if tie in dt (both None count as missing? they compare as equal in the sense of no dt? Or we only compare when both non-None? But the rule says \"choosing the latest event_time, breaking ties...\" - if event_time is missing, how to compare for \"latest\"? Probably treat missing as very old? Or when comparing, if one has dt and other doesn't, the one with dt is considered to have later/earlier? Wait look back at the exact rule: \"keep one row per record_id by choosing the latest event_time, breaking ties by preferring the version with a real net result, then earlier file order.\"\n# So latest event_time - if event_time is missing/empty, it has no date. So when comparing, a row with a real date is always later than one without? Or the opposite? \"Empty event_time means a missing date.\" For determining latest, missing date cannot be latest. So when choosing among versions, prefer the one with non-missing event_time if the other has missing? And among those with non-missing, pick latest.\n# Looking at how we implemented: for two rows, if rdt is not None and bdt is None: pick r. That means prefer row with non-missing event_time - correct, it’s \"later\" in practice. If rdt is None and bdt is not None: keep best - correct. If both None, then event_times are equal (both missing) - so we go to tie-breakers. That matches: \"breaking ties by preferring the version with a real net result, then earlier file order.\" Correct.\n# So for R00212, among those with event_time present, we need to see which one is latest? Let us look at all R00212 with dt present: the latest is 2025-11-29 22:38:10 (idx 2717? let us see the full list). Also there is 2025-11-04 etc. The latest present dt is 2025-11-29 22:38:10 with net 80438.5895 - that's a very large number. Also note the amount_str for that row is '76.513' and after parsing with C1/C2 rules - let us check.\npass\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
