timestamp=2026-11-04T19:49:22.115Z level=INFO run=90d54173 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# with strip: R00212 is no longer in top 10? In prev (no strip) it was at index 7. In new (strip) it's gone. Also R00125 moved up - in new it appears earlier.\n# Let us look at the full ordering and scores roughly? But easier to understand why R00212 dropped. For R00212, the dedup gave us the \"best\" chosen row - which is the one with latest event_time among its versions? Earlier we looked at all R00212 versions. The latest present event_time was 2025-11-29 22:38:10 with net ~80438.59 and channel 'phone\\t'. If we strip channel, then 'phone\\t'.strip() becomes 'phone' (tab is whitespace) -> so channel becomes 'phone'. Then the filter \"channel != \\\"phone\\\" (case-insensitive)\" after normalization means we should exclude it. That means that version of R00212 gets filtered out. That changes everything - the chosen row for R00212 after considering which version survives filters? Or we filter after choosing the best version per record_id?\n# Look at the order of operations in the spec: \"then, after working out net, keep one row per record_id by choosing the latest event_time, breaking ties by...\" followed by \"Keep only rows with non-missing event_time, event_time between ..., channel != \\\"phone\\\" (case-insensitive), and finite net, then within each (region, month) group...\"\n# So deduplication happens FIRST (choose best version per record_id), then filtering is applied to those chosen rows. That means the chosen version must satisfy the filters? Or we filter the pool of rows? The spec says \"Keep only rows with...\" - these are conditions on rows. So filtering is done after deduplication. Also the chosen row for R00212 with latest dt (2025-11-29) has channel 'phone\\t' raw - if channel is treated as 'phone' after stripping, then that chosen row would have channel 'phone' and be filtered out (excluded because channel == 'phone' case-insens). That means R00212 as a record would have no surviving row after filtering? Or another version might be chosen? No - we already chose ONE row per record_id before filtering. That can't be - if the chosen row is filtered out, then R00212 disappears entirely. That can't be right.\n# Or maybe deduplication should happen AFTER filtering? Let us re-read the exact sequence from the task: \"force net = 0 if waive=true, and keep net=NaN if base_amount is NaN, then, after working out net, keep one row per record_id by choosing the latest event_time, breaking ties by preferring the version with a real net result, then earlier file order. Keep only rows with non-missing event_time, event_time between 2025-10-01 00:00:00 and 2025-12-31 23:59:59 inclusive, channel != \\\"phone\\\" (case-insensitive), and finite net, then within each (region, month) group...\"\n# So after computing net for all versions, we deduplicate (pick best version per record_id). Then we filter those chosen rows based on event_time constraints, channel constraint, finite net. That means the chosen representative of each record_id must pass the filters. If it doesn't, that record_id is excluded. That explains R00212's fate if its chosen representative has channel 'phone' after normalization.\n# Is this the intended interpretation? Also look up another record - R00125 is still in top 10. Let us check R00125's chosen representative in our earlier (no strip) computation. R00125 had multiple versions; in the context of filtering by channel, we need to see which version becomes the chosen one and what its channel is.\npass\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
