timestamp=2026-11-04T19:51:50.152Z level=INFO run=90d54173 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# So 77.860 parses as 77860.0. Then net: base 77860, adj -5.21% (so 1+adj = 0.9479), fee +4.90. net = 77860 * 0.9479 + 4.90. 77860 * (1-0.0521): 77860*0.0521 is roughly ~4054, so 77860-4054 ~ 73806, plus 4.90 gives ~73810.99 - matches ~73808. That fits.\n# So the chosen version for R00125 has channel 'email' (no spaces/tabs), so it survives filtering regardless of strip. That explains why R00125 remains.\n# For R00212, the chosen version (latest dt) has channel 'phone\\t'. If we strip, it becomes 'phone' and gets filtered out - so R00212 is excluded entirely. If we don't strip, 'phone\\t' != 'phone' (case-insensitive string compare of raw values), so it does NOT get filtered out - R00212 remains and could end up in results. That gives different outcomes.\n# Which interpretation of \"channel != \\\"phone\\\" (case-insensitive)\" is correct? The literal string \"phone\" - usually we compare ignoring case and ignoring surrounding whitespace. That’s standard. Also the sample data shows variants like ' phone ', 'phone\\t' - they are likely meant to be treated as \"phone\". That suggests we should normalize by stripping whitespace before comparison. Let us check against what gives a plausible \"top 10\" based on typical contest tasks? Or let us look at the other records in the two different top-10 lists.\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
